## Introduction
Atherosclerosis (AS) is a chronic vascular disease caused by the accumulation and deposition of lipids in the subendothelial layer,l that is the major cause of a wide range of severe and fatal cardiovascular diseases， including myocardial infarction and stroke, which have high morbidity and mortality worldwide.[2] As a chronic inflammatory disease driven by lipids,endothelial inflammation is currently recognized as the main initiating mechanism of  As.[3]  Various inflammatory cells,  including macrophages,secrete inflammatoryfactorsintothe microenvironment, which is related to the increase of plaque vulnerability.[4] The production of inflammation often affects the acid-base balance of the body.[5] And the oxidative modification mediated by active species affects the post-translational modification of proteins in cells and tissues, leading to the change of protein function, and ultimately affects the occurrence and development  of AS.[6]  Therefore,  the  research on inflammatory microenvironment is of great significance for the assessment and mechanism study of the AS.
The pH is an important indicator for the study of inflammatory microenvironment, which change is caused by factors such as hypoxia, lactic acid production and a large number of activated macrophages.7] At the same time, inflammation mediates AS from the early stage of the lesion to the late stage of atherosclerotic complications. Protein phosphorylation is the most basic, universal and important mechanism to regulate and control protein activity and function, and the occurrence of inflammation is often accompanied by abnormal levels of phosphorylation.8]  Therefore,  simultaneous  detection  and imaging of pH and phosphorylation levels is of great significance for the assessment of early atherosclerotic disease. At present, the commonly used diagnostic methods for the AS, in addition to conventional blood testing, mainly include intravascular ultrasound (IVUS), intravascular magnetic resonance imaging (IVMRI) and optical coherence tomography (OCT) technology, these methods are all based on the plaque has been formed.[3d.9] There are still challenges for the detection of early plaque formationinAS with themethodsdescribedabove. Fluorescence method has the advantages of high sensitivity, strong specificity and good biocompatibility, moreover， twophoton fluorescence imaging has the advantages of low biological background and greater penetration depth. Therefore, it is of great significance to construct a fluorescent sensor that can simultaneously evaluate pH and phosphorylation in vivo for the assessment of AS.
Herein, we constructed a dual-detection fluorescence nanosensor PCN-NP-HPZ based on MOFs for the simultaneous detection and fluorescence imaging of pH and phosphorylation level in the atherosclerotic mice. Taking PCN-224 as the framework, insert the functional molecule into MOFs through the carboxyl group in the molecule NP-HPZ and used its pHsensitive piperazine group to realize the specific detection of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ The detection of phosphate group was achieved by coordinating with Zr (lV) metal node of the MOFs.We established a model of the early AS by intraperitoneal injection of $\mathsf{V D}_{3}$ combined with high-fat feeding. Then， the blood pH and phosphate were measured in the model mice at different stages. At the same time,according to the blood detection data, the sensor was used for monitoring the pH level and phosphorylation level in the early AS mice with two-photon fluorescence imaging. In order to further verify the performance of the sensor, we also successfully applied the sensor for blood detection and imaging of other inflammatory diseases. lt was found that the blood pH level of mice with early AS was lower than that of normal mice, and the phosphate level was higher than that of normal mice. At the same time, according to the blood test results, we conducted two-photon fluorescence imaging of pH and phosphorylation in mice model of the third week. The data showed that the pH level of mice with early AS was lower than that of normal mice, and the phosphorylation level was higher than that of normal mice. All the results indicated that in the early stages of the AS, both in blood and tissue pH level is lower than normal mice, and phosphate and level of phosphorylation is higher than that of normal mice. The present study provides a new analysis method for assessing the progression of early AS mice using a fluorescence nanosensor for the simultaneous detection and imaging of pH and phosphorylation.
## Results and Discussion
Based on the metal-organic framework PCN-224 and the functional small molecule NP-HPZ, a dual-detection fluorescent nanosensor PCN-NP-HPZ was constructed by simple synthesis strategy at room temperature. We characterized the material by XRD, IR, dynamic light scattering (DSL), SEM and TEM, included the molecular structure of NP-HPZ tested by mass spectrometry and nuclear magnetic resonance hydrogen and carbon spectra (Figure S1 and S2).
TEM and SEM images confirm that PCN-224 in the figure is a spherical nanoparticles with a size of about $140\ \mathsf{n m}$ ，after combining NP-HPZ, the basic size and morphology of the particle do not change (Figure 1a, 1b, 1c, 1d, 1e and 1f). Meanwhile, the DLS experiment showed that the hydration particle size of PCN-224 is about $125\:\mathsf{n m}$ , and the size of sensor PCN-NP-HPZ is about $135~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 1b and 1c). In addition, we also tested the Zeta potential of PCN-224,molecule NP-HPZ and sensor PCN-NP-HPZ respectively, all materials were negatively charged, and the potential of PCN-NP-HPZ was between PCN-224 and NP-HPZ (Figure 1g). Similarly, as shown in infrared spectrum, PCN-NP-HPZ contains all characteristic peaks of PCN and NP-HPZ, confirmed the synthesis of the nanosensor. The XRD test confirmed the frame stability of the material before and after binding NP-HPZ (Figure 1h and 1i).
Then we characterize the optical properties of the sensor. As shown in Figure 2a, in order to study the optical properties of the material, we first performed UV spectrum analysis on PCN-NPHPZ. It is found that the sensor has obvious ultraviolet absorption at $380~\mathsf{n m}$ and $415\ \mathsf{n m}$ ， respectively. And the absorption peak at $380~\mathsf{n m}$ originates from the molecule NPHPZ, moreover, the ultraviolet absorption spectrum of NP-HPZ does not change significantly at pH 5 and 8. The UV absorption peak of porphyrin ligand in PCN-224 is $415\ n m$ ，which is basically consistent with the UV absorption spectrum of the sensor after adding phosphate.
To further study the optical properties of the sensor, we investigated the fluorescence properties of PCN-NP-HPZ with different pH. As shown in Figure 2b and 2c, after the optimization of reaction conditions (Figure S3a and S3c), PCNNP-HPZ has the maximum fluorescence emission peak at 530 nm with the decrease of pH. The fluorescence decreases linearly with the increase of pH, the linear correlation coefficient is 0.9758, and the linear range is $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 4\mathsf{-}\mathsf{9}$ . At the same time, we also made the corresponding response curve, the excitation wavelength was $380~\mathsf{n m}$ ， and the collection range was 450-620 nm. In addition, we tested the fluorescence quantum yield of the sensor for the pH-responsive fluorophore, namely NP-HPZ, to study the luminescence of PCN-NP-HPZ at different pH. The fluorescence quantum yield of the NP-HPZ was the highest at $13.19\%$ . As the pH increased to 7.4 and 8, the fluorescence quantum yield of the material decreased to $3.30\%$ and $2.88\%$ 、 respectively, which was consistent with the fluorescence change of the sensor in response to pH. We also tested the reversibility of the sensor in response to pH, and found that after several cycles, the sensor still had a good response to pH (Figure S4).
Then we tested the sensing properties of PCN-NP-HPZ with phosphate. As shown in Figure 2d and 2e, after optimizing the reaction conditions (Figure S3b),with the increase of phosphate concentration, the sensor had the maximum fluorescence emission peak at $645~\mathsf{n m}$ . And the emission intensity increased linearly with the concentration of phosphate， the linear correlation coefficient was 0.9985. The linear range was 0-200 $\upmu\mathsf{M}$ ，and the detection limit was $3.8~\upmu\mathsf{M}$ .We also made the corresponding fluorescence response curve. The experimental condition of excitation wavelength was $415\:\:\mathsf{n m}$ ，and the collection range was $620–750\ \mathrm{nm}$ .In addition,the fluorescence quantum yield of phosphoresponsive porphyrin in PCN-NP-HPZ was $0.38\%$ under blank condition， and the fluorescence quantum yield increased to $3.30\%$ after the addition of $100~\upmu\upmu$ phosphate, which was consistent with the fluorescence enhancement.
Through the above experimental results, it was found that the sensor PCN-NP-HPZ had good specific response to pH and phosphate. Regarding the response of the sensor to pH,we believed that the N atom in the piperazine group of the sensor molecule NP-HPZ was protonated, which resulted in the fluorescence change of the naphthalene fluorophore. We have carried on the corresponding theoretical calculation (Figure
S5).The specific response of the sensor to phosphate is due to the specific coordination between the $Z r(N)$ metal node of PCN224 and phosphate ion, which interrupts the charge transfer between the ligand and zirconium ion, and finally restores the electron free state of the ligand molecule, leading to the fluorescence intensity of fluorophore porphyrin increase.[10]
Subsequently,we studied the selectivity of the sensor. In order to verify the pH specificity of the sensor PCN-NP-HPZ, we carried out interference tests on different kinds of interferents, including anions, metal ions and various amino acids. The experimental results proved that the sensor only produced corresponding changes in pH under 380 nm excitation wavelength. Moreover, the presence of various interfering substances, including phosphate, does not affect the detection performance of the sensor. In Figure 2f, the values from 1 to 26 are blank, ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}^{3-}$ $(200~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ， $\mathsf{S O}_{3}^{2-}$ $(200~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ， $\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-}$ $(200~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ， $\mathsf{S}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}^{2-}$ $(200~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ， ${\mathsf{C O}}_{3}^{2-}\left(200\upmu\mathsf{M}\right)$ ， ${\mathsf{H C O}}_{3}^{-}$ 一 $(200~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ , Cr (1 mM), I $(1~\mathsf{m M})$ ， $N O_{3}^{-}$ $(1~\mathsf{m M})$ ， $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ （ $100~\upmu\upmu)$ ， $Z n^{2+}$ （ $100~\upmu\upmu)$ ,L-Cys (200 $\upmu\upmu)$ ，Glutamic acid $(200\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ，L-cystine $(200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ ，threonine(200 $\upmu\mathrm{m})$ ，methionine $(200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ ，D-aspartate $(200~\upmu\mathsf{m})$ ，glycine (200 $\upmu\mathrm{m})$ ， L-aspartate $(200~\upmu\mathsf{m})$ ， $O_{2}.$ ” $(5~\mathsf{M m})$ ,GSH (1 mM), ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ (1 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}_{\mathrm{\ell}}^{\star}$ ，tert-butanol peroxide (TBHP) $(5~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ ， $\cdot\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}$ $(5~\upmu\upmu)$ ，NO (5 $\upmu\upmu)$ .The same interference substances mentioned above were also used for the specific detection of phosphate response, and the results showed that our sensor also had a specific response to phosphate. As shown in Figure 2g, the sensor did not respond to any substance except phosphate, so we believe that the sensor PCN-NP-HPZ has a specific response to the phosphate. We then selected L-O-phosphoserine, casein phosphopeptide, and casein phosphorylation products to test whether the sensor could respond to the phosphorylation products, and the results showed that the sensor could respond to these phosphorylation products very well (Figure S6).At the same time,in order to prove that the response of PCN-NP-HPZ to pH and phosphate does not interfere with each other, we did the following experiments: When the pH values were 5 and 6, $200~\upmu\upmu$ phosphate was added， under the excitation of $415\ \mathsf{n m}$ ，the fluorescence of the sensor at $645~\mathsf{n m}$ was almost unaffected by the change of pH and could still respond to phosphate normally (Figure S7). Combined with the experimental results of pH selectivity (Figure 2f),it can be seen that under the excitation of $380~\mathsf{n m}$ ，the fluorescence intensity of the sensor at $530~\mathsf{n m}$ was not affected by phosphate and can still detect pH normally. Therefore, the sensor PCN-NP-HPZ showed good selectivity and not interfere with each other in the determination of phosphate and pH.
We then studied the application of sensors in cells. Prior to cell assays,we demonstrated low cytotoxicity (Figure S8) using CCK-8, and then used PCN-NP-HPZ for imaging intracellular pH and exogenous phosphates in human hepatocytes.As shown in Figure 3a, after adding $30~\upmu\up g/\up m L$ sensor and incubating the cells for $40\mathrm{\min}$ ,the cells were incubated in PBS buffer with different pH values for 40 min in the presence of $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ Mycin of Nigeria[1l Imaging was perfrmed under SP8 laser confocal microscope, and it could be seen that with the increase of pH, the fluorescence intensity in the cells decreased in the range of $435\mathrm{-}620\ \mathrm{\nm}$ . Figure 3b showed that under the same sensor concentration and incubation conditions， the fluorescence intensity of the cells at $620{-}750\ \mathrm{nm}$ increased after the addition of $500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ exogenous phosphate. The above results were consistent with the fluorescence intensity output results of Figure 3c and 3d. Therefore, the sensor PCN-NP-HPZ can respond to pH and phosphate at the cellular level.
In order to study the changes of pH and phosphate levels in atherosclerotic mice，we established an atherosclerotic mice model by intraperitoneal injection of $V_{\mathsf{D3}}$ combined with high-fat feeding,[12] at the same time, serum cholesterol and low-density lipoprotein (LDL) levels were measured at different time points, and it was found that the mice had elevated cholesterol and elevated LDL at the third week of modeling,which indicated that the mice had early symptoms of AS at this time. To verify further model formation, we performed H&E section studies on 9 weeks model mice and normal mice, and the successful establishment of the model was demonstrated by plaque and cholesterol crystallization on the inner wall of blood vessels and slow weight gain or even reduction (Figure 4a, 4c and S9).AS is a chronic inflammation[3c3d] The pH of the inflammatory microenvironment is lower than that of normal tissue[13] The changes of tissue microenvironment often affect the changes of the levels of some active substances in the blood， and the changes of some indicators in the blood can reflect the occurrence of diseases.[14] At the same time, atherosclerotic diseases can cause changes in blood phosphate levels.[13b] Therefore, we expected to assess the disease progression by changes in serum pH and phosphate Then, tail blood samples were collected at 0, 3, 6 and 9 weeks for pH and phosphophosphate fluorescence detection. Before detection, we tested the relative standard deviation of the sensor in detecting pH and the spiked recovery of detecting phosphate, and the results showed that the sensor had excellent performance (Figure S10),the RSD of pH detection was 1.8- $2.5\%$ ， and the recovery rate of phosphate spiked was 95.3- $100.1\%$ . Finally, the results of serum detection showed that the fluorescence intensity of atherosclerotic mice at 3 weeks,6 weeks and 9 weeks was higher than that of normal mice, which indicated that the serum pH was lower than that of normal mice (Figure 4f and 4g). Phosphate test results showed that serum fluorescence intensity and serum phosphate content were higher than those of normal mice at 3, 6,and 9 weeks (Figure 4h and 4i). At the same time in order to verify the accuracy of the results we use the other species of mice model,and at the age of 4 weeks and 8 weeks taking blood for the test, the testing results of cholesterol and LDL and serum pH and phosphoric acid root test results with Km mice serum and weight change is consistent with the test results (Figure 4d, 4e, 4j, 4k, 4l, 4m and S9). Based on the above research,we believe that in the Wistar mice model 3 weeks mice has appeared the symptoms of early AS. To further investigate the changes in pH and phosphorylation levels in mice with early AS,we performed two-photon fluorescence imaging of the arterial walls of mice modeled for three weeks, the test results of inflammatory factors in the serum of mice and the staining results of sections proved the early formation of the disease at this stage, (Figure. S11).
The imaging results showed that compared with normal mice, the imaging fluorescence intensity of pH level in the inner wall of aorta of mice with early AS was higher than that of normal mice (Figure 5a, 5b, 5d and 5e),which indicated that the pH level in the inner wall of aorta of mice with early AS was lower than that of normal mice. lmaging was performed on both thoracic and abdominal aorta and the output intensity was consistent (Figure 5g and 5h). We believe that this is the decrease of pH and abnormal phosphorylation caused by vascular endothelial inflammation.We also two-photon imaged the inner wall of the aorta and found that thefluorescence intensityof phosphorylated images of the aortic wall was higher in mice with early AS than that of normal mice, indicating that the level of phosphorylation in early atherosclerotic mice was higher than that of the normal mice (Figure 5a, 5c, 5d and 5f). Moreover, the results showed that the images output intensity of thoracic and abdominal aorta was consistent (Figure 5g and 5h). Figure 5i and 5j showed the pH and phosphorylation images at different depths of mice aortic inner wall. Figure S12 showed the distribution of sensors in mice by ICP-MS detection of major organs.
To further validate the performance of the sensor and expand its application in other diseases,we also used the sensor for blood detection and imaging in other inflammatory diseases. We selected colitis with the same high incidence as the model study, established Balb/c mice and Wistar mice models of colitis by feeding DSS and enema acetic acid,[15] and proved the establishment of the model by various means such as inflammatory factor detection and section staining (Figure S13,
S14,S15 and S16). Then we used the same method to detect the pH and phosphate in the serum of Balb/c mice. The results showed that the fluorescence intensity of serum pH in the colitis group was higher than that of the normal group, and the fluorescence intensity decreased after treatment, indicating that the serum pH of the colitis group was lower than that of the normal mice, and the pH slightly increased after treatment. The fluorescence intensity of serum phosphate in colitis group was higher than that of normal group, and the fluorescence intensity decreased after treatment, indicating that the serum phosphate level in colitis mice was lower than that of normal mice, and the phosphate level was slightly increased after treatment.Figure 6b showed the serum test results of Wistar mice, which were consistent with those of mice.We believe that the above results are caused by the decrease of serum pH caused by inflammation and the decrease of phosphate absorption caused by intestinal damage in mice. Subsequently,we performed twophoton fluorescence imaging on the colon wall of Balb/c mice, and the imaging results showed that pH fluorescence increased at the lesion site and decreased after treatment, which proved that the pH level at the lesion site decreased (Figure 6d, 6f and 6h). However， the phosphorylation test showed that the fluorescence in the lesion site of colitis mice increased and decreased after treatment, indicating that the phosphorylation level of colitis mice increased (Figure 6e, 6g and 6i), and all the above results were consistent with the output results of fluorescence intensity (Figure 6c).
Based on the above experimental results, we found that the pH level and phosphorylation level in the lesion sites of the two diseases were lower than those of the normal group, and the phosphorylation level in the disease group was higher than that of the normal group. We believe that this is due to the increase of metabolic level caused by inflammation, which leads to the decrease of pH caused by the increase of acidic products,and the abnormal phosphorylation level caused by inflammation. However, only pH results of serum detection remain consistent, which is caused by increased tissue metabolism and acidic products caused by inflammation,while phosphate levels are not consistent, therefore, it is necessary to combine different disease conditions for specific analysis.Moreover, due to the limitation of excitation wavelength, our sensor has not yet been applied to in situ optical imaging of coronary plaques. And the detection of unstable plaques is a challenge for current research However, due to technical limitations， the establishment of unstable plaque model is  still very difficult.[16]  We hope to conduct more meaningful studies on unstable plaques in subsequent work.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, we constructed a dual-detection fluorescence nanosensor PCN-NP-HPZ based on post modification of MOFs for simultaneous detection and imaging of pH level and phosphorylation level in mice with early AS. The nanosensor realized the specific detection of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ by the $\mathsf{p H}$ sensitive group in the molecule NP-HPZ， and the phosphorylation level was detected via the specific coordination between the Zr (lV) metal node in MOFs and the phosphate group. Through blood tests at different stages,we found that the pH level in the blood of mice with early AS was lower than that of normal mice, and the phosphate level was higher than that of normal mice. Tissue imaging experiments showed that the pH level of diseased blood vessels was lower than that of normal mice, and the phosphorylation level was higher than that of normal mice. At the same time, in order to demonstrate the versatility of the sensor,we also successfully used the sensor for blood detection and imaging of other inflammatory diseases. It was found that in colitis, serum pH and phosphate levels were decreased in mice, while in vivo pH levels were decreased and phosphorylation levels were increased. The present study provides a new fluorescent tool for the assessment of early AS progression and study of the mechanism of disease.
